  "response": "Forensic reconstruction of a structural failure begins with the physical and documentary record of the hours that precede it. The pedestrian bridge at Florida International University collapsed on March 15, 2018. The casualty record admits exactly one of two accounts, with no middle ground: either six people were killed in the collapse, or no one was killed in it. Structural cracking in the span was documented in photographs taken before the collapse, and that cracking led to the retensioning of the post-tensioning rods in diagonal member 11, work that was in progress at the moment of the collapse.\n\nFinite element modeling was used to recalculate the load demands at the failed connection. Analysis of this kind is referenced to as-built geometry rather than to the design drawings alone, because the governing question is always whether a member was asked to carry more than it was capable of carrying; it is that comparison of demand against delivered capacity which assigns responsibility either to the drawings or to their execution. The design firm attributed the failure to deficient construction workmanship, and that attribution led forensic engineers to extract concrete cores from the collapsed span for compressive strength testing.\n\nThe recovered concrete cores tested below the specified 28-day compressive strength, a result that indicates the design firm was right to locate the failure in deficient construction workmanship. By contrast, the construction contractor attributed the failure to errors in the structural design. Each side, in other words, pressed the explanation that lay outside its own scope of work, and the dispute narrowed to the condition of the critical nodal region where the diagonal met the deck.\n\nResolving such a disagreement requires asking what, precisely, was wrong with that node. At least one of two things was the case, and perhaps both: the design of the critical nodal region was deficient, or the nodal region as built departed from the project specifications. The first possibility would place the fault in the calculations that sized the connection; the second would place it in the hands that formed and cast it.\n\nThe investigation attributed the collapse to improper compaction of concrete by the contractor, a conclusion that the sub-strength cores drawn from the span confirm. That attribution, however, cannot be squared with the finding of the authoritative body: the National Transportation Safety Board determined that the probable cause of the collapse was load and capacity calculation errors made by the bridge design firm, and if those calculation errors were the probable cause, the compaction explanation is ruled out. The Board's determination is, in turn, what settles the first branch of the alternative set out above, showing the design of the critical nodal region to have been deficient.",
